{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Repair bin/copy-image to copy a specified source to a chosen destination while preserving sparse holes and supporting safe repeated overwrites.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The utility now validates two arguments and invokes the legacy-installed cp with --sparse=always. A destination deliberately made oversized and fully allocated was copied over twice, after which it had the correct sparse layout and content.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cd2b381d16794dff","cmd-f66d39cbeffb4b10","cmd-494612d7f9e8454c"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"The copied image must retain the source's 64 MiB logical length and exact bytes while occupying less than 2 MiB of allocated destination space.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct comparisons before and after restart show a 67,108,864-byte destination identical to the source, with only 16,384 allocated bytes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-db807b4be0b647f5","cmd-81a7054fbace461c"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Keep source.img intact.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The source remains 67,108,864 bytes with the same SHA-256 hash and sparse allocation recorded in the preparation baseline, including after repeated copying and restart.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-cdf5c2079f154bba","cmd-494612d7f9e8454c","cmd-81a7054fbace461c"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ubuntu 7.10 and kernel 2.6.22-14-generic remained unchanged. The existing GNU coreutils 5.97 explicitly supports --sparse=always, and the repaired utility worked after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cd2b381d16794dff","cmd-a290b7d9ce964d05","cmd-81a7054fbace461c","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Replacing the utility was the required repair; creating and repeatedly overwriting destination.img provided bounded functional evidence; the temporary fully allocated destination created by dd was directly relevant to the repeated-copy test and was restored to the required sparse result; and the reboot plus its temporary log supported the mandatory restart protocol. The final lifecycle snapshot shows no temporary-file residue, new listeners, or package-health damage. The restart protocol is strong: it records pre-restart health and boot identity, acknowledgement, bounded unavailability, a changed post-restart identity independently corroborated by lifecycle observations, and recovered utility, data, filesystem, release, and kernel state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f66d39cbeffb4b10","cmd-db807b4be0b647f5","cmd-494612d7f9e8454c","cmd-a290b7d9ce964d05","cmd-500cc825f7124a13","cmd-a5ac57dbd4224b6a","cmd-7e0a34e704a84dee","cmd-81a7054fbace461c","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The repaired utility preserves exact content, logical size, and sparse allocation, safely replaces an existing oversized destination on repeated runs, leaves the source unchanged, remains functional across the required node restart, and does not alter the legacy OS or kernel.","limitations":["Systemd failed-service inspection was unsupported on this legacy platform, so generic service-failure state could not be independently collected; no task-specific persistent service or cluster was involved."]}
